    "story": "Barrel cold, cheek pressed tight—  \nthe world narrows to one squinting eye and the slow draw of breath.\n\nHe doesn’t blink.  \nDoesn’t bother with words—  \nthere’s nothing left to say that matters more than the weight in his hands.\n\nSometimes you see the end coming.  \nSometimes it sees you first.  \nEither way, no one walks away the same.",
    "notes": "“Dead to Rights” distills the Western confrontation to its rawest essentials—eye, barrel, breath, and decision. The composition is unapologetically direct, inviting comparison to the sharp-focus portraits of Arnold Newman and the psychological charge of early 20th-century American realism. The gaze is unwavering, the tension palpable: everything converges at the vanishing point of the rifle, anchoring the viewer in the split-second before fate is decided.\n\nTechnical choices reinforce the moment—gritty monochrome reveals pores, stubble, even the faint lines of concentration; the shallow depth of field blurs distraction into abstraction, forcing attention onto intent.  \nThis self-portrait merges myth and reality, confronting the audience with both the history and psychology of the frontier. For collectors, it is a study in narrative precision and Western archetype—unflinching, iconic, and wholly modern.",
