    "story": "Don’t look nervous.  \nKeep the pan steady.  \nThey can smell worry quicker than they spot gold.\n\nHeard that crack in the brush again—  \nMight be nothing.  \nMight be trouble.  \nDon’t let ‘em see you count your luck out loud.\n\nGold’s shining but don’t stare at it too long.  \nKeep one eye on the prize,  \nthe other on every shadow that moves.  \nNobody strikes it rich without learning who’s behind them first.",
    "notes": "“Eyes on Gold” captures the unease and solitary tension of the Gold Rush era, evoking the observational style of Timothy O’Sullivan and the psychological atmosphere of early Western cinema. The composition is all about watchfulness: low angle, alert posture, hands steady on the pan but attention divided between fortune and threat. Painterly monochrome brings out the textural grit of earth, clothing, and the diffuse, searching light.\n\nTechnically, the image balances sharp focus on the prospector’s wary gaze with a soft, restless background—suggesting both opportunity and ever-present danger. It’s a portrait of vigilance: no gold is ever truly found alone, and every reward comes with a cost measured in shadows.  \n\nIdeal for collectors who appreciate narrative tension and the mythic realities of frontier ambition, this image stands as a meditation on risk, watchfulness, and the price of keeping what you find.",
