    "story": "The train hums under my feet.  \nMetal warm from the sun.  \nSmoke and dust and something sharp in the air  \nthat makes my eyes sting.\n\nThey say I have to go live with Aunt Ethel.  \nSaid it like the tracks already know the way.  \nSeems there’s always somebody  \nwho knows what’s better for me.\n\nI keep thinking they might come back.  \nJust once.  \nLike they forgot something.  \nLike they forgot me.\n\nBut the sun keeps finding my face  \nthrough the dirty window.  \nWarm. Steady.  \nRight now, that’s the only thing that doesn’t leave.",
    "notes": "This work distinguishes itself through point of view and restraint, presenting the Western experience through the interior world of a child rather than action or myth. By placing the viewer inside a moment of departure—felt through sound, heat, and motion—the image transforms the West from a place of adventure into one of memory being formed in real time.\n\nThis painterly handling of light and atmosphere aligns with traditions found in early pictorial photography and narrative illustration, where emotion is carried through texture, silence, and suggestion rather than explicit storytelling. The softened tonal range and controlled contrast allow sensory details—the warmth of sunlit metal, drifting dust, and filtered light—to do the emotional work.\n\nWhat makes this piece especially compelling within contemporary Western art is its focus on what remains after leaving. Rather than celebrating movement or heroism, *Learning Goodbye* honors the quieter legacy of the frontier: the children, witnesses, and moments that absorb loss without language. It is a work that deepens with time, offering collectors an image that resonates as lived memory rather than spectacle.",
